# EVERY CONTRACT THIS REPOSITORY DECLARES, AND WHERE IT DISAGREES WITH ITSELF. One row per
# disagreement, naming the contract it came from. EMPTY IS THE CORRECT OUTPUT.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE CONTRACT NAME IS ADDED AT THE UNION SITE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES ONE FILE ENOUGH.
#    Each roster and each population is tagged with the contract it belongs to as it enters the
#    set, so the difference is taken ONCE over `(contract, subject)` pairs rather than once per
#    contract. Neither `checks/roster.sqlc` nor `arithmetic/roster.sqlc` knows it has a sibling;
#    the caller knows, because the caller is the thing holding both. A member that carried its
#    own label would be answering a question about the set it happens to be in, which is not a
#    fact about the member.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND IT IS WHY THE TWO CONTRACTS NEED NO SHARED SHAPE. They spell their key differently --
#    `rule` against `site` -- and their verdicts differ in TYPE: `violates` is a boolean,
#    `verdict` is three-valued text. Neither difference survives contact with the union, because
#    every branch here only ever asks `IS NULL` of the verdict, and the union site asks the
#    QUESTION rather than carrying the value. `(c.violates IS NULL)` and `(z.verdict IS NULL)`
#    both arrive as booleans. Nothing is renamed into a wrapper relation and nothing is flattened.
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ THE PARENTHESES AROUND EACH ARM ARE LOAD-BEARING. `EXCEPT` and `UNION ALL` share
#    precedence in Postgres and associate left, so `A UNION ALL B EXCEPT C UNION ALL D` parses as
#    `((A ∪ B) − C) ∪ D` and silently adds D back. Written without them this query returned 227
#    rows where 0 was correct -- and 227 well-formed rows is not a thing anybody reads twice.
#    ⭐ Which is why the probe below is not optional: an empty result proves nothing here on its
#    own, because the broken form is also capable of returning one.
#
# ⭐ A THIRD ROSTER IS ONE MORE ARM IN EACH BRANCH. There will be one; the arithmetic roster did
#   not exist yesterday. This is the file that grows, and no other file has to.
#
# ⚠️ THE SECOND BRANCH LOOKS UNREACHABLE AND IS NOT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE
#    SOMEBODY DELETES IT. Every check takes its rule NAME from the roster it joins, so removing a
#    roster row does not produce an undeclared rule -- the check simply stops emitting, and the
#    FIRST branch reports it. The second fires on a different failure: a check that breaks the
#    contract by naming ITSELF, which is what copying a check file and editing it badly produces.
#    Probed by replacing `r.rule` with a literal in one check: both branches fire together, which
#    is the correct reading of that document.
--
-- ⚠️ PROBE BEFORE TRUSTING AN EMPTY RESULT. Add a row to checks/roster.sqlc naming a check that
--    does not exist, recompose, and this must report it against `rules`. Do the same in
--    arithmetic/roster.sqlc and it must report against `arithmetic`. Revert both.
--    algebra/roster.sqlc likewise, reporting against `algebra`. Add a row to
--    diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc naming a table that does not exist and it must report against
--    `diagrams`; drop one and it must report the table as undeclared.
-- checks/, arithmetic/, algebra/ and both diagrams/ contracts: each roster against its population.
SELECT x.contract, 'declared, but nothing produces it' AS problem, x.subject
FROM (
    (
        :compose(algebra/declared_subjects.sqlc)
    )
    EXCEPT
    (
        :compose(algebra/produced_subjects.sqlc)
    )
) x
UNION ALL
SELECT x.contract, 'produced, but nothing declares it', x.subject
FROM (
    (
        :compose(algebra/produced_subjects.sqlc)
    )
    EXCEPT
    (
        :compose(algebra/declared_subjects.sqlc)
    )
) x
UNION ALL
SELECT DISTINCT p.contract, 'examined a row and returned no verdict', p.subject
FROM (
    SELECT 'rules' AS contract, c.rule AS subject, (c.violates IS NULL) AS silent, c.filing
    FROM ( :compose(checks/all.sqlc) ) c
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'arithmetic', z.site, (z.verdict IS NULL), z.filing
    FROM ( :compose(arithmetic/all.sqlc) ) z
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'algebra', w.law, (w.holds IS NULL), w.subject
    FROM ( :compose(algebra/all.sqlc) ) w
) p
WHERE p.silent AND p.filing IS NOT NULL
